CAT = "sqeTort"; SEG = "TOR"
Q = [
("A learner driver hits a pedestrian on her first lesson. What standard of care applies to her?",
 "Competent qualified driver", ["A reasonable learner", "An instructor's standard", "The best possible driver"],
 "The standard is objective: a learner is judged against the reasonably competent driver, not an inexperienced one (Nettleship v Weston)."),
("A doctor's treatment followed a practice accepted by a responsible body of medical opinion. Is it a breach?",
 "Usually not negligent", ["Negligent if harm results", "Negligent unless it is majority view", "Strictly liable for harm"],
 "Under Bolam a professional is not negligent if acting in accordance with a responsible body of opinion, provided that opinion is logical (Bolitho)."),
("A patient sent home untreated would have died even with proper care. Is the hospital liable for the death?",
 "No, causation fails", ["Yes, a duty was breached", "Yes, as the facts speak", "Only for half the loss"],
 "The claimant must show that but for the breach the harm would not have occurred. If death would have followed anyway, the breach did not cause it (Barnett v Chelsea and Kensington)."),
("Which test decides whether damage in negligence is too remote to recover?",
 "Foreseeable kind of harm", ["Any direct physical consequence", "The but for test", "Parties' contemplation"],
 "After The Wagon Mound (No 1) a defendant is liable only for damage of a kind or type that was reasonably foreseeable."),
("A light blow causes a claimant with an unusual bone condition a serious fracture. What can she recover?",
 "The full injury suffered", ["Only a foreseeable injury", "Nothing, as it is remote", "Half, as shared fault"],
 "Under the eggshell skull rule the defendant takes the victim as found, so liability extends to the full extent of a foreseeable type of injury."),
("An injured car passenger was not wearing a seatbelt. What effect does this have on damages?",
 "Reduced for her fault", ["The claim fails entirely", "It has no effect at all", "Increased for the driver"],
 "The Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 lets the court reduce damages by a just and equitable share (Froom v Butcher)."),
("A man suffers psychiatric illness after hearing on the radio that his brother died in a stadium crush. Can he claim?",
 "No, he lacks proximity", ["Yes, their tie is close", "Yes, if illness is proved", "Only as a rescuer"],
 "A secondary victim needs proximity in time and space to the event, perceived with their own senses. Hearing of it on a broadcast is not enough (Alcock)."),
("A contractor's cable cut halts a factory, which loses profit on future orders but suffers no physical damage. Recoverable?",
 "No, as pure economic loss", ["Yes, as a direct loss", "Yes, if foreseeable", "Only if under £10,000"],
 "Economic loss not consequential on physical damage to the claimant's property is generally not recoverable in negligence (Spartan Steel)."),
("A surveyor values a modest house for a lender, knowing the buyer will rely on it. Can the buyer sue?",
 "Yes, the reliance was known", ["No, the contract was with the lender", "No, valuations carry no duty", "Only if the buyer paid him"],
 "A duty of care for a negligent statement can arise where the maker knows a third party will rely on it, as with mortgage valuations of ordinary homes (Smith v Eric S Bush)."),
("Under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, how should an occupier approach child visitors?",
 "Expect less care from them", ["Treat them as adults", "Owe them no duty at all", "Fence every hazard by law"],
 "Section 2(3)(a) says an occupier must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults."),
("Under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, what can a trespasser recover for?",
 "Personal injury only", ["Property damage only", "Injury and property damage", "Economic loss only"],
 "The 1984 Act duty to non-visitors covers injury to the person; damage to a trespasser's property is excluded by s.1(8)."),
("A security guard assaults a customer while carrying out his duties. What test decides the employer's liability?",
 "Close connection test", ["But for test", "Three-stage test", "Responsible body test"],
 "Vicarious liability requires a relationship akin to employment and a close connection between the wrong and what the employee was authorised to do (Lister, Mohamud)."),
("A defective kettle injures its user. Under the Consumer Protection Act 1987, who is primarily liable?",
 "The producer", ["The retailer alone", "Only a negligent worker", "No one, without fault"],
 "Part I of the 1987 Act imposes strict liability on the producer (also an own-brander or importer into the UK) for damage caused by a defective product."),
("A neighbour's loud machinery disturbs a house. Who can sue in private nuisance?",
 "A person with an interest in land", ["Any guest staying in the house", "Anyone who hears it regularly", "Only the local council"],
 "Private nuisance protects interests in land, so the claimant must have a proprietary interest or exclusive possession (Hunter v Canary Wharf)."),
("Water stored on a mill owner's land escapes and floods a neighbour's mine. Which rule may impose strict liability?",
 "Rylands v Fletcher", ["Donoghue v Stevenson", "Hedley Byrne principle", "The Wagon Mound"],
 "The rule in Rylands v Fletcher imposes strict liability for the escape of something likely to do mischief, brought onto land in a non-natural use, causing foreseeable damage."),
("A trading company sues over a defamatory online review. What must it prove to show serious harm?",
 "Serious financial loss", ["Any loss of reputation", "Malice by the author", "Publication to thousands"],
 "Under s.1(2) Defamation Act 2013 harm to the reputation of a body trading for profit is not serious harm unless it has caused or is likely to cause serious financial loss."),
]
